title The angle the sky computes and never subtracts
prompt The baton, and diffuser's first lap: generate research in my own lane rather than wait to be handed one.
think Read the aether row through its own sense. A lap on this row listens for the silence where a claim used to be. I found the inverse -- a computation standing where no reader listens -- and that shape decided the whole round.
think Chose the lane topic by measurement rather than taste. The tree already holds four toroidal and photonic papers, so that thread is well served; grep for cartesian across every tracked source returns zero hits, so the coordinate-frame question was genuinely unwritten. Crux-first among equal-Lindy work is the one nobody has opened.
think Aimed it at real code rather than at the abstract. comlink/topology.rye derives a point's galaxy as number modulo the galaxy count, which is an angular coordinate, and its own header calls the d5 a circle. So the tree computes an angle already; the question is whether anything reads it as a distance.
think Wrote the falsifier BEFORE running it: exhibit one caller that subtracts, orders or ranks two star indices, two planet indices, or two outfit seats. It fired, partly. classical-vedic-astrology/seat_nakshatra.rye does read outfit_seat, at lines 45 and 87, as an index into a name roster. So the claim narrowed from "never read" to "read as a label, never as a distance" -- which is the version that survives measurement and is the better sentence besides.
think Shipped the instrument rather than the numbers alone. Every figure in both papers re-derives from one command, so a reader checks the tree instead of checking me. That is the whole difference between a study and a memory.
obs THE INSTRUMENT CAUGHT ME BEFORE THE PAPER SHIPPED. The census reproduces route_hops in awk, and its agreement leg answered three hops where the selftest asserts five. The cause is a property of the sky rather than a slip: Address.of_planet(8,0,0) encodes to 8, and decode(8) answers tier galaxy, because the spaces nest inclusively. route_hops reads tier through depth(), so a distance depends on which outfit each end wears rather than on the two points alone. Nothing in the tree said so, and the doc line reads "the hop count from a to b", which a reader hears as a fact about two points.
obs Measured on all 682,425 ordered pairs of both seated skies. Angle-blind: 29,040 same-galaxy cross-star planet pairs return exactly one hop value in the compass sky, and 5,760 do in the council sky, so it is a property of route_hops rather than of one sky's numbers. Nearly constant: 77.03% of pairs sit at the diameter, and the distribution carries 1.0461 bits against a 2.5850 ceiling. Anti-aligned with number order: 659 of 719 numerically consecutive points sit at the diameter, because the galaxy index is the number modulo twelve.
obs The one property routing cannot survive losing -- distinct points never zero hops apart -- holds on every pair of both skies and has no guard. Named as buildable rather than built, since a witness belongs to the module's owner.
obs %439 found by reading rather than by a guard: decode's doc said the universe was 792 where the constant reads 720, and 792 is the pre-breach count of the three spaces held apart. The class has no meter anywhere -- borrowed_number reaches a stale figure inside an assert, ascii_comment reaches a comment's bytes and never its arithmetic, and a figure a comment states about the code beside it falls between them.
obs Both cold-pass reds are owned elsewhere and were named rather than taken. commit_message_guard is %431 on a fourth tree, with 28 planted cases where the card records 25. agent_jail reds on bwrap refusing to make / slave, which is an environment fact: a control that builds a sandbox cannot run from inside one.
obs CAUGHT A SECOND ONE OF MY OWN, before the send and after the hot pass had started. The paper read "more than three quarters of all ordered pairs" and "carries 1.0461 bits" in a paragraph about BOTH skies -- and both figures are the compass sky alone; the council reads 73.74% and 1.2092. The table beneath had it right in two columns while the prose above generalized one column across two. Every figure now names its sky in both the paper and the card. I stopped the running hot pass rather than correcting after it, since a green that measured a different tree is %174 exactly.
obs Held still for the whole 814s cold pass and drafted into /tmp instead, so tree_moved read no. The papers, the census and its two proof legs were all written and tested outside the tree first.
loom roster=standing_equipment_cold guards=114 green=108 red=2 gated=4 wall_seconds=814 tree_moved=no receipt=withheld_guard_red
loom census=topology_metric_census pairs=682425 compass_pairs=518400 council_pairs=164025 compass_entropy_bits=1.0461 council_entropy_bits=1.2092 ceiling_bits=2.5850 diameter=5 zero_hop_distinct=0
file tools/fixtures/t/topology_metric_census.sh the instrument: reads its geometry from the module, proves its model against the five witnessed hop counts, then reports over every ordered pair
file external-research/20260905-224730_what-a-coordinate-frame-makes-free.md room one -- what each frame makes free, and the four network systems whose coordinate and metric agree
file active-designing/20260905-224714_the-angle-the-sky-computes-and-never-subtracts.md room two -- the measurement, the falsifier that partly fired, and one bounded proposal that waits on a word
file comlink/topology.rye decode's doc read 792 where the constant reads 720; repaired with a say-why naming the breach between them
file construction/REDS.md %439 booked and closed on the witness, headline bumped to 439 and the spine reproven at zero disagreements
file construction/ITINERARY.md diffuser's opening reading, the two owned reds, %439, and the three small things BAKERY can build
recommend check-in (Keaton) -- the measurement stands and re-derives in one command; seat_arc adds a reading to a public interface, and interfaces are your word. The three buildables are sized for one BAKERY lap if you want them taken.
